WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:04.680
You hand an agent your project. In one minute, it changes twenty files.

2
00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:08.985
Most of the time, that's exactly what you wanted. Sometimes it isn't.

3
00:00:09.485 --> 00:00:15.365
Git is how you get back. It keeps your project's history as a series of save points.

4
00:00:15.365 --> 00:00:17.325
Each one is called a commit.

5
00:00:17.825 --> 00:00:21.258
Monday. First version of the page. Save.

6
00:00:21.258 --> 00:00:24.176
Tuesday. Add a contact form. Save.

7
00:00:24.176 --> 00:00:27.523
Tuesday night. Change the colors. Save.

8
00:00:28.023 --> 00:00:31.856
Wednesday, you ask the agent to rework the layout. It does.

9
00:00:31.856 --> 00:00:32.831
And it's worse.

10
00:00:33.331 --> 00:00:37.427
No problem. You step back to Tuesday night's save point.

11
00:00:37.427 --> 00:00:41.012
Everything is exactly as it was. Nothing is lost.

12
00:00:41.512 --> 00:00:44.478
GitHub keeps a copy of that whole history online.

13
00:00:44.478 --> 00:00:48.231
It's your backup, and it's where your app gets published from.

14
00:00:48.731 --> 00:00:54.301
So the habit is simple. Save before the agent does anything big.

15
00:00:54.301 --> 00:00:56.737
Like the result? Save again.

16
00:00:56.737 --> 00:00:58.043
Don't? Go back.
